{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nThe one S3-specific failure that causes real data loss is **multiple bundles for one ref**.\nA live experiment this session reproduced it: when two `<sha>.bundle` objects exist for the\n`tickets` ref, `git ls-remote` returns the ref at two SHAs and a fresh clone **hard-fails**\nwith `multiple updates for ref not allowed` — the store becomes unclonable. The push path also\nstalls: git-remote-s3's `cmd_push` **rejects while `len(bundles) > 1` before writing anything**\n(verified in the installed source, `remote.py` — it returns\n`multiple bundles exists on server` prior to any bundle create/put), and rebar's\n`src/rebar/_store/push.py:58` `_is_non_fast_forward` classifies that as retriable, but the\nsubsequent fetch cannot resolve the ambiguous ref, so retries never converge.\n\nUpstream ships `git-s3 doctor` (`git_remote_s3.Doctor`), but it is unusable for an autonomous\nfleet, verified against the installed library (v0.3.2): `Doctor.fix_multiple_bundles` is\n**interactive-only** (blocking `input()` prompts at `manage.py:137,141`, which crash on EOF) and\nit **discards** the non-chosen head — i.e. it *loses* every event unique to that head. rebar's\nstore is an append-only event log where each divergent head may carry distinct ticket events,\nso discarding a head is unacceptable.\n\nPrior art for healing a forked append-only log is unanimous (restic `check`/`repair`, git-annex\nunion branch, jj op-log, Fossil/Dolt): **merge, never discard**, and put the storage-specific\nsurface behind a thin seam so the merge logic is written once. rebar already embodies this for\nordinary git remotes: `src/rebar/_store/sync.py` `reconverge()`/`_union_merge` reconciles\ndivergent `tickets`-branch heads by a **lossless multi-parent `git merge`** (both parents kept;\nconflict → abort → keep local → hint `rebar fsck-recover`; the only shared mutable file\n`.bridge_state/*` resolves via the tickets-branch `.gitattributes` `merge=ours` documented at\n`src/rebar/_commands/init.py:55` `_GITATTRIBUTES`; UUID-named event dirs never collide, which is\nexactly why the forbidden `merge=union` driver is unnecessary).\n\n**This story reduces the S3 case to the already-solved git case.** The multi-bundle state is\nordinary ref divergence *hidden at git-remote-s3's bundle-storage layer*. The only genuinely\nS3-specific action is unavoidable: because the helper refuses every ref op while `bundles > 1`,\nrebar must issue S3 object writes/deletes directly to collapse the bundle count. Those writes —\nplus bundle enumeration and the repair lock — are performed **through git-remote-s3's own\nimportable library** (its `S3Remote`, its `.s3` boto3 client, its `acquire_lock`/`release_lock`,\nits bundle key layout), so rebar owns no credential chain, key-layout, or lock code, and adds\n**no** direct S3 SDK dependency to core rebar (boto3 ships transitively with the helper, under\nthe optional `[s3]` extra only).\n\n## Approach\n\nAdd a thin **optional** module `src/rebar/_store/s3_doctor.py` exposing\n`heal_multi_bundle(base_path, remote, ref)`, invoked from `src/rebar/_store/push.py` when the\nmulti-bundle signature is detected, then retry the push. The module imports `git_remote_s3`\n**lazily inside its functions**, guarded by the existing `require_s3_helper()` availability/\nversion check (mirroring `_optional.py`), so importing rebar never requires the helper or boto3.\n\n**Detection (push.py).** Add a narrow `_is_multi_bundle(stderr)` predicate matching the two\nobserved signatures — `multiple bundles exists on server` (helper reject) and\n`multiple updates for ref` (ambiguous fetch/clone). In `push_tickets_branch`'s retry loop, at\nthe existing `if not _is_non_fast_forward(stderr):` seam, branch to the doctor **once** (a\nsingle `healed_once` guard prevents an infinite heal→retry loop) *before* the normal non-FF\nrecovery, then `continue` the loop to re-push.\n\n**Heal algorithm — write-merged-tip-first, delete-originals-second, never discard.** All S3\naccess is via the helper's own `S3Remote` (constructed from the configured `s3://` URL with\n`parse_git_url`), so credentials/SSE-KMS come from the boto3 chain the helper already owns. The\nordering is dictated by two verified helper facts: (i) `cmd_push` cannot write while\n`bundles > 1`, so the merged bundle is written by a **direct `put_object`**, not the helper's\npush; (ii) a git-remote-s3 bundle is a plain `git bundle create` object stored at\n`f\"{prefix}/{ref}/{sha}.bundle\"`, which rebar reproduces exactly.\n\n1. **Serialize with the helper's own per-ref lock** — `S3Remote.acquire_lock(ref)` (its\n   `IfNoneMatch=\"*\"` conditional-put, 60s TTL, stale-lock steal, `remote.py`); on `None`/`412`\n   another repair is in flight → back off and let the normal retry re-detect. `release_lock`\n   in a `finally`. **No reinvented rebar `DOCTOR` marker.**\n2. **Re-enumerate under the lock (TOCTOU guard, jj pattern)** — `get_bundles_for_ref(ref)`; if\n   now ≤1 bundle, another doctor already converged → release and return.\n3. **Fetch every head locally** — for each remaining bundle, fetch its tip into a scratch ref\n   (`refs/rebar-doctor/<sha>`) so all divergent commits are present locally.\n4. **Union-merge losslessly via rebar's shared merge primitive** — create a single multi-parent\n   merge commit `T` over all fetched heads with `git merge` under the tickets-branch\n   `.gitattributes` (the **same lossless semantics `sync._union_merge` uses**, factored into one\n   shared primitive both call). **No `merge=union` driver.** On an unexpected conflict in a\n   non-derived file: `merge --abort`, release the lock, leave the remote untouched, and surface\n   `rebar fsck-recover` (keep local state) — never drop a parent. `T` reaches every event from\n   every head.\n5. **Write `T` as a NEW bundle object (durability barrier)** — `git bundle create` for `T`, then\n   `S3Remote.s3.put_object(Bucket=remote.bucket, Key=f\"{prefix}/{ref}/{T_sha}.bundle\", Body=…)`.\n   Remote now holds N+1 bundles, one of which (`T`) is fully lossless. **Nothing has been\n   deleted yet**, so no event can be lost. (If `T_sha` equals an existing head — already-merged\n   — the put is idempotent.)\n6. **Delete the N original bundles** via `S3Remote.s3.delete_object`, leaving exactly `T`.\n   **`T` is never deleted.**\n7. **Point HEAD at `T`** via the helper's `init_remote_head(ref)`, then **release the lock** and\n   delete the local scratch refs (on both the success and abort paths).\n\nThe caller's existing non-FF recovery reconciles the local HEAD against the now-single bundle\n`T` (the ordinary git path — no new code): the retried `git push` fetches the unambiguous `T`,\nmerges, and pushes, so the final bundle carries local writes plus every divergent head.\n\nIdempotency/recovery, aligned to the ordering above: `T` is content-addressed, so re-running\nyields the same tip. Crash invariant at **every** point: because `T` is written before any\ndelete and never deleted, at least one bundle carrying the full merged history is always present\nremotely — a crash before step 5 leaves the N originals (all events present); a crash mid step 6\nleaves `T` plus a subset of originals (`T` alone is lossless, and `bundles > 1` simply\nre-triggers the heal); a crash after step 6 leaves exactly `T`. No fencing token is needed — S3\nstrong read-after-write plus the conditional-put lock make the worst case \"multi-bundle again,\"\nitself healable (Kleppmann: this lock guards *efficiency*, not correctness). Running the heal\nlazily on the next push that hits the anomaly (jj defer-repair / WAL replay-on-startup) means\n**no daemon**. Each heal emits one structured log record (`ref`, the merged head SHAs, the\nresulting `T`, and the deleted bundle keys).\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_store/s3_doctor.py` — NEW optional module: `heal_multi_bundle`, lazy\n  `git_remote_s3` import behind `require_s3_helper()`, the lock→enumerate→fetch→merge→write→\n  delete flow, scratch-ref cleanup, structured logging, and reuse of the shared merge primitive.\n  No rebar-owned boto3/credential/key-layout/lock code beyond the one bundle key string the\n  helper defines. Target 200–500 LOC; hard cap 800 (split along call-graph seams only).\n- `src/rebar/_store/push.py` — add `_is_multi_bundle`, a `healed_once`-guarded branch to\n  `heal_multi_bundle` at the non-FF seam, and the post-heal push retry.\n- `src/rebar/_store/sync.py` — factor the existing lossless multi-parent merge (currently inside\n  `_union_merge`) into a shared primitive the doctor reuses (behaviour-preserving; sync's own\n  oracle must stay green).\n- `tests/` — new test module (see Testing).\n\n## Testing\n\n- Tier **real temporary git repositories** (git refs / merge / reachability / data loss).\n  Oracle: construct a ref with two divergent bundles, each carrying a **unique** commit; run\n  `heal_multi_bundle` against an **in-process fake `S3Remote`** (models `acquire_lock`/\n  `release_lock`/`get_bundles_for_ref`/`init_remote_head` and a `.s3` with `put_object`/\n  `delete_object`/`list_objects_v2` over a local object dir — no real AWS, no new hard test dep);\n  assert (a) exactly one bundle remains for the ref, (b) a fresh clone succeeds, and (c) **every**\n  unique commit from **both** heads is reachable from the healed tip (collateral invariant: no\n  unique commit dropped) — the exact loss upstream's discard would cause.\n- Tier **conflict path** (non-derived-file conflict). Oracle: two heads that conflict in a\n  non-derived, non-`.bridge_state` file; assert the heal aborts, deletes **nothing** remotely\n  (all original bundles intact), releases the lock, and surfaces the `fsck-recover` hint — never\n  dropping a parent.\n- Tier **multi-process locking race** (repair storm). Oracle: N real processes hit the\n  multi-bundle state behind a shared barrier against the shared fake; assert exactly one performs\n  the merge (the rest get `None` from `acquire_lock` and back off) and the final state is a\n  single healed bundle with full reachability.\n- Tier **crash-recovery** (kill after failpoint). Oracle: inject a failure **after** the merged\n  bundle is put but **before/among** the original deletes; assert `T` is present, the store is\n  still lossless, and the next detection re-heals to a single bundle (idempotency) — the\n  failpoint matches the write-then-delete ordering.\n- Tier **push-path integration**. Oracle: seed the `multiple bundles` stderr signature and assert\n  `push_tickets_branch` auto-invokes the heal exactly once (the `healed_once` guard) and then\n  converges (no infinite non-FF retry loop).\n- **Opt-in live canary** (`tests/e2e/test_s3_doctor_live.py`, AWS-credential-gated, skipped in\n  credential-less CI) exercises the happy path against a real ephemeral bucket to validate the\n  real conditional-write/multi-bundle semantics the fake models, and pins the git_remote_s3\n  v0.3.2 API surface the heal depends on (`parse_git_url`, `S3Remote` construction,\n  `acquire_lock`/`release_lock`, `get_bundles_for_ref`, `.s3`, `init_remote_head`).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] After `heal_multi_bundle` on a ref with two divergent bundles, exactly one bundle remains,\n      a fresh clone succeeds, and every unique commit from both heads is reachable from the\n      healed tip. Verified by the real-git-repo oracle.\n- [ ] The heal never discards a head/event: a commit unique to the non-chosen head is present in\n      the healed history (the specific regression upstream's interactive doctor causes). Verified\n      by the reachability assertion.\n- [ ] The heal writes the merged tip before deleting any original bundle and never deletes the\n      merged tip, so at every crash point ≥1 bundle carrying the full merged history remains\n      remotely; a mid-delete crash re-heals to a single lossless bundle on the next run. Verified\n      by the crash-recovery oracle.\n- [ ] On an unexpected non-derived-file conflict the heal aborts without deleting any remote\n      bundle, releases the lock, and surfaces `rebar fsck-recover` — no parent dropped. Verified\n      by the conflict-path oracle.\n- [ ] Concurrent doctors do not corrupt or duplicate-repair: with N processes behind a barrier,\n      exactly one merges (the rest observe the helper's held per-ref lock and back off) and the\n      final state is a single healed bundle. Verified by the multi-process race oracle.\n- [ ] `push_tickets_branch` auto-invokes the heal on the multi-bundle signature exactly once and\n      the push converges afterward (no infinite non-FF retry loop). Verified by the push-path\n      test.\n- [ ] The heal keeps append-only semantics and adds **no** direct S3 SDK dependency to core\n      rebar: all S3 access (lock, enumerate, put, delete, HEAD) is via git-remote-s3's own\n      `S3Remote`/`.s3` imported lazily behind `require_s3_helper()`; no `merge=union` driver is\n      used; the helper lock TTL is unchanged (default 60s). Verified by inspection and by rebar\n      importing with the `[s3]` extra absent."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786210849617321001,"uuid":"223ede0a-65dc-483c-8e5a-60ec674e84c6"}